    "result_text": "My `riskFlag` change is complete and verified. Here's the summary.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec: return `riskFlag` true when **any** of \u2014 owner is a possible duplicate, email domain is disposable-adjacent, or city is over its soft capacity \u2014 otherwise false.\n\nThree changes, following the existing derived-field pattern:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `riskFlag` boolean (read-only) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../util/RiskFlags.java`** (new) \u2014 the logic:\n   - **possible duplicate** \u2192 `possibleDuplicate == true` (the soft-match flag already stored on create).\n   - **city over soft capacity** \u2192 `capacityWarning == true` (already stored; true when the city held 40\u201349 owners, i.e. past the soft threshold of 40, the hard cap of 50 being rejected outright).\n   - **disposable-adjacent email** \u2192 the email's second-level domain label matches a known disposable provider (`mailinator`, `tempmail`, `guerrillamail`) on a *different* full domain, e.g. `mailinator.net` \u2014 distinct from the hard blocklist (`mailinator.com` etc.), which is rejected at create time.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 a `@Mapping` expression computing `riskFlag` from the owner's stored `possibleDuplicate`, `capacityWarning`, and `email`, so it's consistent on both the create response and later GETs.\n\n## Test results\n\n`Cp58Tests` (the riskFlag checkpoint) **passes**, along with 67 others: **68 of 70 green**.\n\nThe remaining 2 failures \u2014 `Cp23Tests` and `Cp39Tests`, both asserting `membershipLevel` 3 but getting 2 \u2014 are **pre-existing and unrelated to this task**. They concern the household membership-level *capping* logic (`cappedMembershipLevel`), which my change doesn't touch. I verified this empirically: I temporarily reverted all three of my edits and ran those two tests against the clean baseline \u2014 they failed identically (`expected:<3> but was:<2>`). Fixing them would mean altering the membership-capping feature, which is outside this change's scope and risks other checkpoints.\n\nSo the suite is in exactly the same state as before, plus the new `riskFlag` checkpoint now passing. I did not touch the membership logic to avoid masking or entangling that separate, pre-existing conflict \u2014 let me know if you'd like me to dig into those two as well.",
